# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from vals import *
import tables

vv = V["valid"]
cl = V["conv_lap"]
pr = V["prod"]
en = V["energy"]
qs = V["qs"]
Tpk = 310.0 + 14.974
kdpk = TUM["A"] * math.exp(-TUM["Ea"] / (RG * Tpk))
selpk = (TUM["A"] / HM["A"]) * math.exp((HM["Ea"] - TUM["Ea"]) / (RG * Tpk))
alpha1 = 0.778 / (1660.0 * 2540.0)
td = 0.004 ** 2 / alpha1
cm = C["common"]
S1 = lambda n: OM["S1_" + n]["best"]
S2 = lambda n: OM["S2_" + n]["best"]
pareto1 = [OP["S1_cov%.1f" % c]["best"]["m"]["JF"] * 1e3 for c in (0.3, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0)]
pareto2 = [OP["S2_cov%.1f" % c]["best"]["m"]["JF"] * 1e3 for c in (0.3, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0)]
js = [r["J"] for r in OM["S1_MGL"]["per_np"]]
jsall = [r["J"] for n in ("Pennes", "CV", "MGL") for r in OM["S1_" + n]["per_np"]]

H1("4. Results and discussion")
H2("4.1 Verification of the numerical solution")
P("The first set of results concerns the accuracy of the finite element solution, because every later statement depends on it. The comparison with the Laplace-domain reference is made for the linear problem (constant perfusion), for a single 2 s pulse of $3\\times10^{5}$ W/m², at the times 1, 3, 6 and 12 s and at nine positions from the surface to 16 mm. "
  "For all four heat models the relative difference between the finite element and the reference temperature, normalised by the largest reference value at that time, is below " + pct(max(max(vv[n]["eth"][1:]) for n in vv), 2) + " per cent from 3 s on, and the in-plane stress agrees to " + pct(max(max(vv[n]["esg"][1:]) for n in vv), 2) + " per cent (Figure 2 and the upper block of Table 4). "
  "At 1 s, while the pulse is on, the differences are larger: " + pct(max(vv[n]["eth"][0] for n in vv), 2) + " per cent in temperature and " + pct(max(vv[n]["esg"][0] for n in vv), 2) + " per cent in stress for the worst model, which is the MGL case. "
  "This is the expected consequence of starting the second-order backward differentiation scheme from the rest state when the source switches on at $t=0$; the error decays as the solution becomes smooth. The inversion error of the reference itself, measured by the difference between the accelerated and the plain Fourier sum, is below " + pct(max(max(vv[n]["trunc"]) for n in vv), 1) + " per cent.")
FIG("Figure_2.png", "Verification of the finite element (FE) solution against the Laplace-domain reference for the linear problem with a 2 s pulse of 3×10^5 W/m². (a) Temperature histories of the MGL model at the surface, at the interface and at 8 mm. (b) Interface temperature for the Pennes, CV and MGL models. (c) In-plane stress profiles of the MGL model at 3 s and 6 s. FE and reference curves coincide at the resolution of the figure; the quantitative errors are in Table 4.")
P("The convergence study (Figure 3) separates the time-step error, the mesh error and the quadrature error of the burn integral. Against the Laplace reference the temperature error falls from " + sc(cl["Pennes"]["edt"][0], 1) + " at $\\Delta t=0.32$ s to a floor of about " + sc(cl["Pennes"]["edt"][-1], 1) + " that is set by the spatial discretisation and the inversion. The decrease is not monotone between 0.16 s and 0.04 s, because errors of opposite sign from time stepping and from the mesh partly cancel. "
  "A cleaner view is given by the self-convergence of the nonlinear pulse-train problem with temperature-dependent perfusion and damage, using a reference with half the mesh size and a quarter of the time step. The minimum tumour damage integral converges in time with an error of " + sc(pr["dt"][0][1][1], 1) + " at 0.8 s, " + sc(pr["dt"][1][1][1], 1) + " at 0.4 s, " + sc(pr["dt"][2][1][1], 1) + " at 0.2 s and " + sc(pr["dt"][3][1][1], 1) + " at 0.1 s, an observed order between 1.8 and 2.3. "
  "The collateral integral $J_F$ is the most sensitive quantity to the mesh: its error is " + pct(pr["dx"][0][1][3], 2) + " per cent at $\\Delta x=1$ mm, " + pct(pr["dx"][1][1][3], 2) + " per cent at 0.5 mm, " + pct(pr["dx"][2][1][3], 2) + " per cent at 0.25 mm and " + pct(pr["dx"][3][1][3], 2) + " per cent at 0.125 mm, which is second-order convergence. "
  "The search mesh (0.25 mm) therefore resolves $J_F$ to better than 1 per cent, and the final designs are evaluated at $\\Delta t=0.05$ s. ")
FIG("Figure_3.png", "Convergence of the numerical method. (a) Time-step error against the Laplace reference (linear problem) and against a refined solution (pulse-train problem with nonlinear perfusion). (b) Mesh error for the same cases. (c) Error of the Arrhenius burn integral on a linear temperature ramp for composite Simpson with 2, 4 and 8 panels per step and for the trapezoidal rule applied to the rate.")
P("The quadrature of the burn integral is exact at constant temperature to rounding error (relative error below " + sc(max(V["quad"]["const"]) * 1.0001, 1) + "), as it must be since the rate is then constant. On a ramp of 0.5 K/s over 60 s with the tumour kinetics, which spans a rate change of several orders of magnitude, Simpson's rule with four panels per step has the error " + sc(V["quad"]["ramp"]["rows"][0][1][1], 1) + " at $\\Delta t=10$ s, falling with fourth-order behaviour to " + sc(V["quad"]["ramp"]["rows"][2][1][1], 1) + " at 2.5 s, whereas the trapezoidal rule applied to the rate has the error " + pct(V["quad"]["ramp"]["rows"][2][1][3], 2) + " per cent at 2.5 s and only second-order convergence. "
  "At the production step of 0.05 s the quadrature error is therefore negligible against the other errors. The energy balance, Eq. (@energyb@), closes to " + sc(en["Pennes_linear"]["rel_res"], 1) + " of the incident energy for the Pennes model and to " + sc(en["MGL_linear"]["rel_res"], 1) + " for the MGL model in the linear problem, and to " + sc(en["Pennes_nonlinear"]["rel_res"], 1) + " and " + sc(en["MGL_nonlinear"]["rel_res"], 1) + " with the nonlinear perfusion law. "
  "The incident energy computed from the load vector agrees with the analytic integral of the Gardner profile to " + sc(en["Pennes_linear"]["rel_src"], 1) + ". For the CV model the storage functional used for the balance is incomplete, because the flux relaxation stores energy that it does not include, and the residual of " + sc(en["CV_linear"]["rel_res"], 1) + " is a property of that functional; it is not reported as a check.")
H2("4.2 Quasi-static limit and the character of the thermoelastic stress")
P("With inertia retained in the Laplace-domain solution, the elastic transit time is " + sc(qs["twave"], 2) + " s, and the ratio of Eq. (@epsin@) for a loading time scale of 1 s is " + sc(qs["ratio_sq"], 1) + ". The explicit comparison at the four output times shows that the temperature with the true density differs from the temperature with the density reduced by a factor of $10^{4}$ by at most " + sc(max(r["dth"] for r in qs["rows"]), 1) + " relative to the peak, which is at the level of rounding in the inversion, and the axial stress with inertia retained never exceeds " + sc(max(r["ratio"] for r in qs["rows"]), 1) + " of the in-plane stress. "
  "Reducing the density by a factor of $10^{2}$ instead of $10^{4}$ gives the same result to the same accuracy, so the effect is not a numerical artefact of the reduction. The quasi-static limit is therefore justified quantitatively for loading times of the order of a second and longer. "
  "It would not be justified for nanosecond or microsecond pulses, for which the elastic wave propagation produces genuine axial stress and pressure transients, and the present model should not be used for such pulses.")
P("The same calculation shows that the thermoelastic coupling is negligible for the heat balance. The coupling term raises the effective heat capacity by 0.016 per cent in the tumour and 0.06 per cent in the normal tissue, so that the Pennes, CV and MGL temperature fields are insensitive to whether the coupling is kept. "
  "The stress is a consequence of the temperature field and not the other way round. Because the axial stress vanishes, the stress constraint is applied to the in-plane stress of Eq. (@sig@). This quantity is proportional to the local temperature, with the factor $s_i$, so that the stress limit is in effect a limit on temperature and on its rate of change, with a larger weight in the stiffer normal tissue. "
  "At the interface the stress on the normal side, with $s_2=" + f1(LC[1]["sl"]) + "$ Pa/K, is " + f2(LC[1]["sl"] / LC[0]["sl"]) + " times larger than on the tumour side for the same temperature, which is why the peak stress of every design occurs on the normal side of the interface. A caveat is that the in-plane stress assumes complete lateral constraint. The stress in a real lesion, whose lateral extent is finite, is smaller and depends on the geometry. "
  "The values reported are therefore an upper bound under the stated assumption.")
H2("4.3 Baseline optimal design")
P("The optimization results for the four conduction models in the two scenarios are collected in Table 2. For the MGL model in the selective scenario S1 the best design found has " + str(int(bd["Np"])) + " pulses of " + f2(bd["tp"]) + " s with a period of " + f2(bd["per"]) + " s and a peak intensity of " + f1(bd["I0"] / 1e3) + " kW/m², which is a duty cycle of " + f2(bd["tp"] / bd["per"]) + ", a nearly continuous exposure of " + f1(bd["Np"] * bd["per"]) + " s with a total fluence of " + f2(bd["I0"] * bd["Np"] * bd["tp"] / 1e6) + " MJ/m². "
  "The peak temperature, at the surface, is " + f1(bm["Tmax"] - 273.15) + " °C, well below the vaporization limit. The peak in-plane stress is " + f1(bm["sabs"] / 1e3) + " kPa, compressive and on the normal side of the interface, below the limit of 36 kPa. The tumour is killed (damage fraction at least 0.99) over its full depth, with the constraint active at the tumour edge, where the minimum damage integral is " + f3(bm["minOmT"]) + " against the requirement " + f3(math.log(100)) + ". "
  "Near the surface the damage integral is of order 100, so that the tissue next to the laser is far more damaged than required; the profile is a result of the exponential optical decay. The damage fraction on the normal side of the interface is " + f3(FN_h) + ", and it falls below 1 per cent at 2.25 mm from the interface. The burn integral in the normal tissue never reaches 1, so that no healthy tissue reaches the 63 per cent damage level, and the collateral measure is $J_F=" + f3(bm["JF"] * 1e3) + "$ mm.")
TAB(*tables.T2())
FIG("Figure_4.png", "Baseline optimal design for the MGL model in scenario S1. (a) Temperature histories at five depths. (b) Damage fraction profiles at three times; the damage keeps growing after the laser is off. (c) In-plane stress on both sides of the interface and at the surface, with the stress limit.")
P("A property of the solution which is easy to miss is that almost all of the damage is done after the laser has been switched off. At the end of the pulse train, at " + f1(bd["Np"] * bd["per"]) + " s, the damage integral at the tumour edge is only " + f3(0.01936) + ", about 0.4 per cent of its final value of " + f3(bm["minOmT"]) + ". "
  "The temperature at the tumour edge peaks at " + f1(14.97) + " K above the reference, which is " + f1(310 + 14.97 - 273.15) + " °C, at 28.6 s, some 15 s after the end of the exposure, because heat from the hot surface layer conducts through the tumour on a time scale of tens of seconds. "
  "This feature is a consequence of the one-dimensional geometry, which has no lateral heat loss and cools only by conduction into the normal tissue and by perfusion, and it is the reason why the evaluation horizon matters so much in the sensitivity analysis. A laser spot of finite diameter would lose heat laterally and would have a shorter tail. "
  "In the model the evaluation horizon $t_e=120$ s captures most but not all of the damage; the minimum tumour damage integral of the baseline design increases from " + f2(C["horizon"][0]["minOmT"]) + " at 30 s to " + f2(C["horizon"][1]["minOmT"]) + " at 60 s, " + f2(C["horizon"][2]["minOmT"]) + " at 120 s and " + f2(C["horizon"][3]["minOmT"]) + " at 300 s, with the collateral integral growing in parallel.")
H2("4.4 Why the pulse structure hardly matters")
P("The most striking feature of Table 2 and of Figure 6a is that the best objective is nearly independent of the number of pulses. For the MGL model, the best values for $N_p=1$ to 12 lie between " + f4(min(js)) + " and " + f4(max(js)) + ", and over the Pennes, CV and MGL models the full range of the best values is " + f4(min(jsall)) + " to " + f4(max(jsall)) + ", a spread of " + f1(100 * (max(jsall) / min(jsall) - 1)) + " per cent. "
  "The optimal intensity, pulse width and period differ greatly from one pulse number to another, but the objective hardly changes, so the objective surface has a broad flat ridge and the design problem is far from having a unique minimiser in the design variables. "
  "The reason can be stated in terms of two time scales. The thermal diffusion time of the tumour layer is")
EQ("t_d=\\frac{h^2}{\\alpha},\\qquad\\alpha=\\frac{k}{\\rho c_v}", "td")
P("which is " + f1(td) + " s for $h=4$ mm and $\\alpha_1=" + sc(alpha1, 3) + "$ m²/s. Pulse periods of a few seconds are short against $t_d$, so the temperature at the tumour edge does not see the individual pulses but only the delivered fluence, smoothed by diffusion. The dose at the edge is delivered along the slow decay of the temperature, as the estimate")
EQ("\\Omega_T(h,t_e)\\approx k_{d,T}\\!\\left(T_h^{\\max}\\right)\\Delta t_{\\mathrm{eff}}", "dose_est")
P("shows, in which $T_h^{\\max}=" + f1(Tpk - 273.15) + "$ °C is the peak temperature at the edge, $k_{d,T}$ is the tumour rate constant, which is " + f3(kdpk) + " s$^{-1}$ at that temperature, and the effective duration is $\\Delta t_{\\mathrm{eff}}=" + f1(bm["minOmT"] / kdpk) + "$ s. This duration is of the order of $t_d$, and it is set by the geometry, not by the pulse train. "
  "The requirement $\\Omega_T(h)\\ge\\Omega_c$ then fixes the peak temperature at the edge, and with it the energy to be delivered, for any pulse train that is short compared with $t_d$. The collateral damage follows from the same temperature history through the selectivity ratio at the edge. "
  "At the peak temperature the rate ratio of Eq. (@sel@) is " + f1(selpk) + " in favour of the tumour, and the ratio of the burn integrals at the interface is the inverse of a similar number,")
EQ("\\frac{\\Omega_N(h,t_e)}{\\Omega_T(h,t_e)}\\approx\\varrho_{\\mathrm{sel}}^{-1}\\!\\left(T_h^{\\max}\\right)", "selratio")
P("which is " + f3(1 / selpk) + " from the estimate against " + f3(bm["OmN_h"] / bm["minOmT"]) + " from the computation, the small difference arising from the temperature range over which the dose accumulates. The collateral objective is therefore set principally by the temperature at the edge and by the kinetic selectivity, and the freedom in the pulse train changes it only at the per cent level. "
  "The practical meaning is that, within this model, the choice of the pulse number and period can be made on other grounds, for example the peak temperature at the surface or the stress rate, because it does not alter the collateral damage; and the designs found by the search differ in those secondary quantities. "
  "The designs differ in secondary quantities: the nearly continuous design has a peak intensity of " + f1(bd["I0"] / 1e3) + " kW/m² and a surface peak of " + f1(bm["Tmax"] - 273.15) + " °C, whereas an equally good pulsed design with $N_p=2$ and a duty cycle of 0.65 has " + f1(OS["base"]["best"]["des"]["I0"] / 1e3) + " kW/m² and " + f1(OS["base"]["best"]["m"]["Tmax"] - 273.15) + " °C, with the same objective to within 0.3 per cent. I do not claim that any of them is clinically preferable.")
H2("4.5 Do the conduction models change the safe dose?")
P("The answer depends on which of the models is used and on the size of the relaxation parameters. Table 3 shows the response of the pulse train that is optimal for the Pennes model when it is applied under each model. "
  "Under CV and MGL the minimum tumour damage integral at the unscaled intensity is " + f2(cm["CV"]["unscaled"]["minOmT"]) + " and " + f2(cm["MGL"]["unscaled"]["minOmT"]) + " against " + f2(cm["Pennes"]["unscaled"]["minOmT"]) + " for Pennes, so the constraint is met with a small margin, and the multipliers that make the constraint exactly active are " + f3(cm["CV"]["scale"]) + " and " + f3(cm["MGL"]["scale"]) + ". "
  "For the MGT-type model with $\\eta=0.5$ the same design leaves the tumour edge with only " + f2(cm["MGT"]["unscaled"]["minOmT"]) + ", a clear failure of the kill constraint, and the dose must be raised by " + f1(100 * (cm["MGT"]["scale"] - 1)) + " per cent, at which point the collateral integral is " + f3(cm["MGT"]["scaled"]["JF"] * 1e3) + " mm against " + f3(cm["Pennes"]["scaled"]["JF"] * 1e3) + " mm for Pennes, a factor of " + f2(cm["MGT"]["scaled"]["JF"] / cm["Pennes"]["scaled"]["JF"]) + ". "
  "The peak stress at that dose is " + f1(cm["MGT"]["scaled"]["sabs"] / 1e3) + " kPa and a short tensile phase appears, which is absent in the other models.")
TAB(*tables.T3())
P("The cross evaluation of the model-specific optima confirms the picture. The Pennes optimum gives a tumour-edge damage integral of " + f2(C["cross"]["Pennes"]["MGL"]["minOmT"]) + " under MGL, whereas the MGL optimum gives " + f2(C["cross"]["MGL"]["Pennes"]["minOmT"]) + " under Pennes, which is below the requirement of " + f2(math.log(100)) + " by 2 per cent, so that planning with the wrong model among these three leads to a shortfall or to an excess of a few per cent. "
  "The MGT optimum, planned with the thermal-displacement term, gives " + f2(C["cross"]["MGT"]["MGL"]["minOmT"]) + " under MGL, an overdose of more than a factor of two in the damage integral. The response of the safe dose to the relaxation time is shown in Figure 5b. "
  "For CV the multiplier falls from " + f3(C["tau_sweep"]["CV"][0]["scale"]) + " at $\\tau_q=0$ to " + f3(C["tau_sweep"]["CV"][-1]["scale"]) + " at 20 s, and for MGL it first decreases to " + f3(C["tau_sweep"]["MGL"][3]["scale"]) + " at 2 s and then rises to " + f3(C["tau_sweep"]["MGL"][-1]["scale"]) + " at 20 s. "
  "The effects are within about 4 per cent for any relaxation time in the range of one to twenty seconds. For the MGT model, Figure 5c, the multiplier rises with $\\eta$ from " + f3(C["eta_sweep"][0]["scale"]) + " at $\\eta=0$ to " + f3(C["eta_sweep"][-1]["scale"]) + " at $\\eta=0.9$. The thermal-displacement term thus changes the dose by up to 12 per cent, and the collateral integral much more.")
FIG("Figure_5.png", "Influence of the conduction model on the dose. (a) Interface temperature histories for the pulse train that is optimal under Pennes (parameters in Table 3) under the four models. (b) Multiplier on the intensity that exactly meets the kill constraint against the relaxation time. (c) The same multiplier for the MGT-type model against its thermal-displacement coefficient.")
P("The reason for the large effect of the thermal-displacement term, in contrast with the small effect of the relaxation times, can be read from the structure of the equations. The relaxation times enter through $\\tau\\partial_t^2\\theta$ and its analogues, whose size relative to the capacity term is $\\tau/t_c$ for a loading time scale $t_c$; with $\\tau=2$ s and a heating and decay process lasting tens of seconds this is a correction of a few per cent. "
  "The term $k^*\\partial_x^2v$ is an integral over the history of the temperature, and in the slow decay phase it acts as an additional, long-lived conduction of heat, so that a model with it distributes heat more widely. The result suggests that the choice of the MGT parameter, and not the relaxation time, is the model-form uncertainty that matters for the design. "
  "It also shows that the source value of $k^*$ cannot be combined with a relaxation time of seconds, as explained in Section 2.5. The comparison between models here is a comparison of mathematical models with illustrative parameters, and no model is claimed to describe real tissue better than another, since no measurements were available.")
H2("4.6 Pareto front and activity of the constraints")
P("The epsilon-constraint sweep of the kill depth (Figure 6b) shows the trade-off between the depth of tumour that is guaranteed to be killed and the damage to normal tissue. In the selective scenario S1 the collateral integral $J_F$ increases from " + f4(pareto1[0]) + " mm for 30 per cent of the tumour depth to " + f4(pareto1[-1]) + " mm for the full depth, a factor of " + f1(pareto1[-1] / pareto1[0]) + ". "
  "The relation is convex in the sense that the last 10 per cent of depth costs " + f1(100 * (pareto1[-1] - pareto1[-2]) / pareto1[-1]) + " per cent of the total collateral damage. Without kinetic selectivity (S2) the damage is larger by one to two orders of magnitude, from " + f3(pareto2[0]) + " mm to " + f3(pareto2[-1]) + " mm, and the front has a similar shape. "
  "The values for S2 show how much of the protection of the healthy tissue in S1 is provided by the assumed kinetic difference and how much by the thermal design. In S2 the burn integral at the interface equals the tumour value by construction, so the collateral damage is determined by the temperature field alone, and the relative cost of the last increment of depth is smaller (a factor of " + f1(pareto2[-1] / pareto2[0]) + " between the ends of the front, against " + f1(pareto1[-1] / pareto1[0]) + " in S1).")
FIG("Figure_6.png", "Optimization results. (a) Best objective for each pulse number and conduction model in scenario S1; the surface is almost flat in N_p. (b) Pareto front of the integrated collateral damage against the guaranteed kill depth for the MGL model in the selective (S1) and non-selective (S2) scenarios. (c) One-at-a-time sensitivity of the minimal objective; blue bars are feasible optima and red bars would denote optima that violate a constraint.")
P("The constraint activity at the base optimum is as follows. The kill constraint is active. The vaporization constraint is far from active, with a margin of " + f1(100 - (bm["Tmax"] - 273.15)) + " K, because the exposures that minimise the collateral damage are gentle. The treatment-time constraint is not active either, since the nearly continuous exposure takes " + f1(bd["Np"] * bd["per"]) + " s against the allowed 30 s, and sweeping the allowed time from 10 s to 60 s changes the optimum by less than 1 per cent. "
  "The stress constraint is the interesting one. A sweep over the stress limit from 26 to 36 kPa and over treatment-time limits of 10, 30 and 60 s shows that the problem is infeasible for limits below about 29.3 kPa for all three treatment times: at 28 kPa the least violating design exceeds the limit by 4.9 per cent and at 26 kPa by 12.8 to 13.0 per cent, and the peak stress found is 29.3 kPa in all cases. "
  "The reason is again the physics of the tail. The stress at the interface at the time of the temperature peak is $s_2\\theta_h^{\\max}=" + f1(LC[1]["sl"]) + "\\times" + f2(14.97) + "=" + f1(LC[1]["sl"] * 14.97 / 1e3) + "$ kPa, and the temperature rise at the edge cannot be lowered below about 15 K if the damage integral is to reach its target within the horizon of 120 s, whatever the pulse train. "
  "A longer horizon would permit a lower and longer temperature plateau and hence a lower stress; with $t_e=300$ s the peak stress of the optimum is indeed " + f1(OS["Teval=300s"]["best"]["m"]["sabs"] / 1e3) + " kPa. The strain-rate lag changes the picture. With $\\tau_1=5$ s and 10 s the optimum violates the 36 kPa limit by " + f1(100 * OS["tau1=5"]["best"]["v"][2]) + " and " + f1(100 * OS["tau1=10"]["best"]["v"][2]) + " per cent, with peak stresses of " + f1(OS["tau1=5"]["best"]["m"]["sabs"] / 1e3) + " and " + f1(OS["tau1=10"]["best"]["m"]["sabs"] / 1e3) + " kPa, because a long lag amplifies the stress that is associated with the temperature rate at the end of the exposure.")
H2("4.7 Sensitivity")
P("Table 4 and Figure 6c give the effect of changing one input at a time, with the optimization repeated each time. Sorting by the size of the effect, the Arrhenius parameters dominate. Raising the frequency factor of the normal tissue by a factor of ten raises the minimal collateral objective by " + f1(100 * (OS["AfacN=10"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent and lowering it by ten reduces it by " + f1(-100 * (OS["AfacN=0.1"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent. "
  "The tumour kinetics matter almost as much: a change of the tumour activation energy to $5.0\\times10^{5}$ J/mol raises $J$ by " + f1(100 * (OS["EaT=5.0e5"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent and to $6.0\\times10^{5}$ J/mol lowers it by " + f1(-100 * (OS["EaT=6.0e5"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent, and halving or doubling the anchor time changes it by " + f1(100 * (OS["trefT=1800s"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " and +" + f1(100 * (OS["trefT=7200s"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent. "
  "The conclusion is that the absolute values of the collateral damage in this paper cannot be taken as predictions, because they depend on poorly known kinetic parameters in an exponential way; the qualitative results, such as the flatness in the pulse number, the dominance of the post-exposure tail and the small difference between Pennes, CV and MGL, are the robust ones.")
P("The evaluation horizon is the next most important input, with " + f1(100 * (OS["Teval=60s"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent for 60 s and +" + f1(100 * (OS["Teval=300s"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent for 300 s, which reflects the tail discussed above. The optical properties come next. A 25 per cent higher absorption coefficient reduces $J$ by " + f1(-100 * (OS["mua x1.25"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent, which is consistent with a shallower deposition of the light, but at the same time the surface becomes hotter (" + f1(OS["mua x1.25"]["best"]["m"]["Tmax"] - 273.15) + " °C against " + f1(bm["Tmax"] - 273.15) + " °C). "
  "A thinner tumour (3 mm) raises $J$ by " + f1(100 * (OS["h=3mm"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent, consistent with the normal tissue being closer to the surface, whereas a 5 mm tumour reduces it by " + f1(-100 * (OS["h=5mm"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent. "
  "Perfusion has a small effect: raising it tenfold lowers $J$ by " + f1(-100 * (OS["wbscale=10"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent, and removing it changes $J$ by +" + f1(100 * (OS["wbscale=0"]["best"]["J"] / OS["base"]["best"]["J"] - 1)) + " per cent. The shutdown of perfusion after damage has no visible effect at all, because the tumour is damaged only in the tail when the temperature is already falling, and the normal tissue is hardly perfused. "
  "The relaxation time $\\tau_0$ changes $J$ by less than 4 per cent over the range 0.5 s to 10 s. The strain-rate lag $\\tau_1$ does not change $J$ at all, because it enters the stress and not the heat balance, but it changes the stress and so decides the feasibility.")
TAB(*tables.T4())
H2("4.8 Limitations and honest assessment")
P("The study has several limitations that bear on the interpretation. There is no experimental or clinical validation: the model has been verified against an independent mathematical solution, against exact results and against an energy balance, which shows that the equations are solved correctly, but whether the equations describe a real tumour has not been tested. "
  "The Arrhenius parameters of the tumour are an anchored illustration, and the Henriques–Moritz values, for which the skin-burn origin is classical, are a proxy for healthy tissue. The perfusion law, the relaxation times and the stress limit are illustrative. "
  "The geometry is one-dimensional, so that lateral heat loss is absent, the tail of the temperature is long, and the in-plane stress is a constrained upper bound. A three-dimensional model of a focal spot would change the quantitative conclusions, and the dependence on the pulse structure might become stronger for small spots. "
  "The linear mesh and the second-order time stepping resolve the problem as shown, but the first second of a pulse starting from rest carries an error of the order of one to two per cent. The optimization is a derivative-free local search from random starts; the flat ridge of the objective means that the minimiser is not unique and that the reported designs are representative members of a family, not the single best design. "
  "Finally, the stress limit and the vaporization limit are not active at the minimum of the collateral damage, with the exception of the infeasibility below 29.3 kPa, and a reader who needs them to be active would have to specify them from tissue data. None of these results is a clinical recommendation.")

H2("4.9 Interpretation for treatment planning")
P("The results can be translated into statements about planning, within the limits stated above. The first statement is that the dose should be specified at the tumour edge and not at the surface. The surface temperature in the optimal designs is about 60 °C, but the tumour is killed only because the edge, at about 52 °C, remains warm for a long time; a plan that is based on the surface temperature alone would not see that the edge is the critical point. "
  "The second statement is that the time after the exposure is part of the treatment. The tissue at the edge receives nearly all of its damage after the laser is off, so that the time the tissue spends above about 48 °C, and not the duration of the exposure, determines the outcome, and a plan has to include the cooling phase in its predictions. In a real situation, with lateral heat loss and with convective cooling of the surface, this phase would be shorter, and the model would then call for a higher fluence. "
  "The third statement concerns the use of pulses. The pulse structure was found to be nearly irrelevant for the collateral damage; this should not be generalised, because the tumour in the model is thick compared with the pulse period and the heating is weak enough not to approach the vaporization limit. For thin tumours, for strongly absorbing layers or for higher peak temperatures, the pulse structure would matter through the surface temperature and the stress rate, which are the quantities that the model does show to depend on it.")
P("A fourth statement concerns the margin to the limits. The vaporization limit has a margin of about 40 K at the optimum, and the stress limit of 36 kPa has a margin of about 20 per cent, but the latter is an illustrative value and the stress is calculated under complete lateral constraint. The feasibility threshold of 29.3 kPa shows that a stress limit and a kill requirement can be incompatible for a given horizon, and a plan should check this compatibility before an optimization is attempted. "
  "The fifth statement is about model risk. The difference between Pennes, Cattaneo–Vernotte and modified Green–Lindsay predictions of the safe dose is small compared with the uncertainty that comes from the kinetic parameters, which can change the collateral damage by a factor of several. The effort of a treatment-planning study should therefore be spent first on the kinetic parameters of the tumour and of the surrounding tissue, and only afterwards on the choice among the conduction models. "
  "The exception is the thermal-displacement term of the Moore–Gibson–Thompson type, which changes the dose by up to twelve per cent, and for which neither the form nor the coefficient is established for biological tissue; if this term is of interest, it should be examined with transient temperature measurements that can separate it from the relaxation time of the heat flux.")
